{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792870703,"nonce":"v6CBtzz+M8xiThUrBfRNvqT4FDg=","title":"Native O2 misses the pure-recursion transform an interpreted start gets","message":"On the fibonacci benchmark (naive `fib(40)`), an interpreted start's tier-up -- PassManager::startup (O0 with tree fusion) followed by after_required(O2) -- returns from main in about 9 ms. A native start, `for_level(O2)` from raw MIR, takes about 122 ms in main, and disabling `pure-recursion` there changes nothing, so the pass never fires on the native pipeline. Disabling `tail-recursion` instead slows it to about 275 ms.\n\nThe difference is pass order: the startup pipeline runs O0's copy propagation, DCE and scalar replacement before O2's PureRecursionPass sees the function, and O0 no longer expands self-recursion first. Find what PureRecursionPass needs that raw MIR lacks and run it (or its prerequisites) in for_level(O2) as well, so `--tier-start-interpreted false` gets the same code.\n\nMeasured on the NUC with RAYZOR_STARTUP_TIMES, cold cache, both arms interleaved.","files":null}]}